1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,000
On Earth a flame points up, because hot gas rises. On the Space Station there is no up.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,500
A burning droplet turns into a sphere, burns slowly, and can survive in air that would put it out on the ground.

3
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:19,500
We are DIU Smart X from Dhaka, and this is LUMEN, our entry for Flame in Freefall.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:26,000
Our team has 4 members. [Member 1] leads the machine learning.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
[Member 2] built the interface. [Member 3] handles the NASA data.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
[Member 4] leads the pitch and documentation.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,500
We are students at Daffodil International University, competing at the Dhaka local event of NASA Space Apps 2026.

8
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:53,000
The challenge, Flame in Freefall, asks us to apply AI to decades of microgravity combustion research and turn it into fire-safety insight for spacecraft.

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:01,000
NASA has run hundreds of droplet burns on the station. The results sit in tables that a flight engineer cannot read in a hurry.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:08,000
We also wanted to avoid the trap this challenge sets: a chat window over some PDFs is not a fire-safety tool.

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,500
So the question we answer is simple. Given a chamber atmosphere, how will this flame end?

12
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:23,000
Our data is NASA's PSI-69 record: the Flame Extinguishment Experiment, FLEX, from the Physical Sciences Informatics repository listed on data.nasa.gov.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The table holds 274 station burns. 261 of them carry a pressure, a droplet diameter and a labelled ending.

14
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,000
The seven features are oxygen, nitrogen, carbon monoxide and helium mole fractions, chamber pressure, droplet diameter, and the fuel: methanol or heptane.

15
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The three endings are extinction, disruption and completion.

16
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,500
The whole table and the trained model are committed to the repository, so anyone can rerun the fit.

17
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:57,000
This is LUMEN running in a browser, fully offline. The model is a gradient-boosted classifier trained on that table.

18
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
It is exported to JSON and evaluated inside the page. No server, no network call.

19
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:08,500
We start with a recorded station test, 20CAL10. Methanol, 21 percent oxygen, one atmosphere.

20
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:14,000
The station recorded extinction. The model says extinction, with 87 percent confidence.

21
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:20,500
Now test 20CAL08: nearly the same atmosphere, a slightly smaller droplet. The station saw the droplet disrupt.

22
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:25,500
The model agrees: disruption, 74 percent.

23
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Here is the moment that matters. We lower oxygen one percent at a time.

24
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Twenty-one percent: disruption. Twenty percent, and the prediction flips to extinction at 92 percent.

25
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
By 16 percent the model is certain the flame goes out.

26
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,500
One percent of oxygen is the boundary between a flame that breaks apart and a flame that dies.

27
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Push oxygen back up to 30 percent and disruption dominates again.

28
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Pick the 34-percent, low-pressure, large-droplet test, and the model expects a completed burn, as the station recorded.

29
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:05,000
We report the score honestly. Five-fold cross-validated accuracy is 75 percent.

30
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Always guessing extinction, the most common ending, scores 69 percent.

31
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Recall is 88 percent for extinction, 60 for disruption, and only 22 for completion, which has just 27 rows.

32
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:20,500
Permutation importance shows what the model actually uses: oxygen first, then droplet diameter, pressure, and fuel.

33
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:24,500
That matches the physics, which is the check we wanted.

34
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:31,000
Why it matters. A spacecraft atmosphere is a design choice: oxygen fraction, diluent, pressure.

35
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
LUMEN gives an engineer a one-second answer grounded in real station data, with its own held-out score printed next to the result.

36
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:49,500
Next we add the BASS and ACME datasets, an out-of-range warning, an uncertainty display, and a retrieval layer that cites the exact experiment behind each prediction.

37
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:55,500
Everything is open source under Apache 2.0, with every dataset cited. The code is at github.com/[your-org]/lumen.

38
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:59,500
We are DIU Smart X. Thank you.

